WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:03.800
Cloud IAM is not only about employees signing into a console.

00:00:04.020 --> 00:00:06.260
Workloads also need identity.

00:00:06.480 --> 00:00:09.280
CI/CD pipelines deploy infrastructure.

00:00:09.500 --> 00:00:12.140
Containers call cloud APIs.

00:00:12.360 --> 00:00:14.760
Virtual machines retrieve configuration.

00:00:14.980 --> 00:00:17.220
Serverless functions process events.

00:00:17.440 --> 00:00:20.640
Kubernetes workloads read secrets or publish messages.

00:00:20.860 --> 00:00:26.540
Applications use service accounts, managed identities, workload identities, tokens,

00:00:26.760 --> 00:00:30.520
or deployment roles to perform tasks without a human clicking a button.

00:00:30.740 --> 00:00:33.940
Non-human identities often create the largest blind spots.

00:00:34.160 --> 00:00:37.920
A shared service account may be used by several applications,

00:00:38.140 --> 00:00:40.780
so no one knows which system caused an action.

00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:45.240
A deployment role may be overpowered because it was created to make automation easy.

00:00:45.460 --> 00:00:49.780
A leaked access key may remain active because no owner is tracking it.

00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:54.080
A workload in development may accidentally have access to production resources.

00:00:54.300 --> 00:00:57.900
These problems are not exotic; they are normal engineering drift.

00:00:58.120 --> 00:01:02.840
Safer patterns include workload identity federation, managed identities,

00:01:03.060 --> 00:01:08.100
short-lived credentials, narrowly scoped deployment roles, environment separation,

00:01:08.320 --> 00:01:11.040
secret rotation, and clear ownership.

00:01:11.260 --> 00:01:15.100
A build pipeline should have the permissions needed to deploy its application,

00:01:15.320 --> 00:01:17.080
not broad rights across the account.

00:01:17.300 --> 00:01:22.260
A serverless function should read the specific data it needs, not every storage resource.

00:01:22.480 --> 00:01:27.040
A Kubernetes workload should not share one powerful identity with unrelated workloads.

00:01:27.260 --> 00:01:29.020
Auditability is essential.

00:01:29.240 --> 00:01:32.280
Automation should leave a clear trail of which principal acted,

00:01:32.500 --> 00:01:36.660
which pipeline or workload used it, what changed, and who owns the system.

00:01:36.880 --> 00:01:38.480
When a production change happens,

00:01:38.700 --> 00:01:42.860
responders should not have to guess whether it came from a user, a deployment tool,

00:01:43.080 --> 00:01:46.200
a managed identity, or a compromised key.

00:01:46.420 --> 00:01:50.980
Workload identity hardening is how cloud teams make automation fast without making it

00:01:51.200 --> 00:01:51.840
opaque.

00:01:52.060 --> 00:01:54.700
Environment separation is a practical guardrail.

00:01:54.920 --> 00:01:58.920
A development pipeline should not normally deploy to production unless that is its

00:01:59.140 --> 00:02:00.420
explicit job.

00:02:00.640 --> 00:02:03.760
A test service account should not read production secrets.

00:02:03.980 --> 00:02:08.860
A serverless function in one application should not write to another team's storage.

00:02:09.080 --> 00:02:13.320
These boundaries make mistakes easier to contain and ownership easier to understand.

00:02:13.540 --> 00:02:16.900
Workload identity design should be part of application architecture.

00:02:17.120 --> 00:02:21.600
When a new service is created, teams should ask which cloud APIs it needs,

00:02:21.820 --> 00:02:24.700
which resources it can touch, how credentials are delivered,

00:02:24.920 --> 00:02:28.680
who owns the identity, how logs identify its actions,

00:02:28.900 --> 00:02:30.820
and how access will be removed later.

00:02:31.040 --> 00:02:35.440
Answering those questions early is far cheaper than cleaning up an overpowered service

00:02:35.660 --> 00:02:37.820
account after production launch.

00:02:38.040 --> 00:02:41.720
Automation should have the same review discipline as human access.

00:02:41.940 --> 00:02:47.220
If a pipeline is allowed to create networks, assign roles, or deploy privileged functions,

00:02:47.440 --> 00:02:49.440
reviewers should understand why.

00:02:49.660 --> 00:02:53.740
The fact that a principal is non-human does not make it lower risk.

00:02:53.960 --> 00:02:55.720
In many cloud environments,

00:02:55.940 --> 00:02:59.620
workload identities are among the most powerful identities in the account.
